  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nConsider the following:\n\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<iframe src=\"a/subdoc.html\"></iframe>\n<script>\nonload = () => {\n  document.querySelector(\"iframe\").contentWindow.location.href = \"#foo\";\n};\n</script>\n```\n\nPer [spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-location-href) this uses the entry settings object, which means that this will navigate the page to the _parent_ document's URI (with the `#foo` hash).\n\nThis makes some amount of sense if you're using a relative URI, but it is unlikely to be what the author wanted in this case, and it could even happen in relatively innocent-looking code (because the entry global is not always obvious).\n\nThis is what's going on in [Mozilla bug 1996823](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1996823) where (due to another series of somewhat tangential issues) the page actually ends up with an entry global that of the parent document in Firefox but not in other browsers (because the page fails to hook various callbacks in Chromium / WebKit).\n\nIt generally seems really weird that such a navigation would use the entry global. Maybe we should special-case hashes to not use the entry global URI or something? Not sure.\n\ncc @annevk @zcorpan @smaug---- @mfreed7 ",
